1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,702
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,763 --> 00:00:10,120
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:10,850
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,911 --> 00:00:14,525
Welcome to Unit 5: Mixtures and Solubility.

5
00:00:15,586 --> 00:00:18,869
Orange juice, oatmeal and smoothies are all mixtures.

6
00:00:18,869 --> 00:00:23,368
Stir sugar into tea, and it dissolves, until the tea can't hold any more.

7
00:00:23,368 --> 00:00:29,509
In this unit, we'll explore pure substances, mixtures, how to separate them, and how things dissolve.

8
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:35,920
Everything around us is matter, and it can be sorted into pure substances and mixtures.

9
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:38,960
Pure substances are either elements or compounds.

10
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:45,344
An element, like gold or neon, can't be broken down by chemical reactions. There are 118 known elements.

11
00:00:45,344 --> 00:00:51,242
A compound forms when elements join chemically in a fixed ratio, like water, H2O, or salt, NaCl.

12
00:00:51,242 --> 00:00:57,140
Its properties are different from its elements, and it can only be separated by chemical methods.

13
00:00:58,201 --> 00:01:03,186
A mixture contains substances that are not chemically joined, in any proportions,

14
00:01:03,186 --> 00:01:08,962
so they can be separated by physical methods. Mixtures are either homogeneous or heterogeneous.

15
00:01:10,023 --> 00:01:15,313
In a heterogeneous mixture, 'hetero' means different: the parts are not evenly spread,

16
00:01:15,313 --> 00:01:20,177
and you can see them. Soil, sand and water, and oil and water are heterogeneous.

17
00:01:21,238 --> 00:01:26,831
In a homogeneous mixture, 'homo' means the same: the parts are evenly spread, in one state,

18
00:01:26,831 --> 00:01:31,270
and you can't tell them apart. Salt water, air and steel are homogeneous.

19
00:01:32,331 --> 00:01:38,046
A solution is a homogeneous mixture. The substance that dissolves, like sugar, is the solute.

20
00:01:38,046 --> 00:01:42,119
The substance that does the dissolving, like water, is the solvent.

21
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:48,227
A pure substance contains only one type of atom or molecule. Mixtures aren't pure,

22
00:01:48,227 --> 00:01:54,246
but we can describe the purity of one part: how much of it there is compared to the whole mixture.

23
00:01:54,246 --> 00:01:59,171
Bottled water often contains added minerals, so it isn't 100 percent pure water,

24
00:01:59,171 --> 00:02:00,934
but it's still safe to drink.

25
00:02:01,995 --> 00:02:06,494
We calculate percentage purity like this: the mass of the pure substance,

26
00:02:06,494 --> 00:02:09,717
divided by the total mass of the mixture, times 100.

27
00:02:09,717 --> 00:02:13,851
A bronze coin has a mass of 75 grams, and 62 grams of it is copper.

28
00:02:13,851 --> 00:02:17,377
62 divided by 75, times 100, is about 82.7 percent copper.

29
00:02:18,438 --> 00:02:23,181
Purity matters: medicines need exactly the right amount of active ingredient,

30
00:02:23,181 --> 00:02:27,376
and dental implants use very pure metals to avoid allergic reactions.

31
00:02:28,437 --> 00:02:34,578
Jewellers describe the purity of gold in carats. Here is the percentage of gold in four common types.

32
00:02:35,638 --> 00:02:38,618
24 carat gold is almost pure: 99.9 percent gold.

33
00:02:38,618 --> 00:02:44,029
18 carat gold is only 75 percent gold; the rest is other metals such as copper or silver.

34
00:02:45,090 --> 00:02:51,109
Pause and decide: a ring of 18 carat gold has a mass of 8 grams. What mass of gold does it contain?
